{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And of Levi he said.</b> On account of Levi he said, and he would speak with the Holy One, blessed be He. And he said <b>to Your devout one.</b> Referring to Aaron his brother. <b>Whom You tested at Massah.</b> In the sense of trial, or a place-name.<br><b>You strove with him at the waters of Meribah.</b> That is the sin of the Rock, in which no other sin was found. And he did not mention his sin in the matter of the Calf, and this indicates that his intention was solely for the sake of Heaven; for had he sinned in it, he would have mentioned it here together with the sin of the waters of Meribah. And the fact that he placed the Tummim before the Urim — whereas the way of the Torah is always to place the Urim first, because they are the principal names — but he placed them first to indicate that these are not without those, for both of them together constitute the perfection of the matter. And I have already explained their significance in its proper place; and that is the view of the Ramban, of blessed memory, that it was a degree of the degrees of the holy spirit (ruach ha-kodesh), and the priest clothed in the breastplate upon which the Urim and Tummim were placed between its folds would be clothed in these through the holy spirit. From this Onkelos translated תומיא ואוריא אלבשתא — 'he clothed himself' — in the manner of what is written (I Chronicles 12:19): ״ורוח לבשה את עמשי״ — 'and a spirit clothed Amasai.'" "<b>And know</b> that there are four degrees in prophecy: bat kol, Urim and Tummim, holy spirit (ruach ha-kodesh), prophecy; and all of them are distinct levels, one above the other, and all of them are drawn from the attribute called 'righteousness' (צדק). And this is what is written (Job 29:14): ״צדק לבשתי וילבשני״ — 'Righteousness I put on, and it clothed me.'" "<b>And behold, I</b> shall explain to you here these four degrees. The first is bat kol. And there is a mystery in what they said: bat kol and not ben kol — and likewise in what they said, 'this builds a father' (זה בנה אב), and likewise 'a building of a father from two verses' (בנין אב משני כתובים), and they did not say 'this builds a mother' or 'a building of a mother'; and likewise in what they further said, 'there is a mother for the reading (מקרא) and for the tradition (מסורת),' and they did not say 'there is a father' — for all of this was said with precise providence and surpassing wisdom. And the masters of truth, all of whose words are founded on the pillars of wisdom and the sphere of their decree, received this from the prophets back to Moses from Sinai. And the explanation of the matter is that the expression 'building of a father' (בנין אב) is not mentioned anywhere in the Talmud except regarding a verse from the verses of the written Torah, and if so, it is fitting for them to use the language of 'father' in this context and to say 'this builds a father' or 'a building of a father,' for every single verse is the building of the Father, may He be exalted, whose Torah we heard from His mouth from within the fire — as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:15): ״ביום דבר ה׳ אליכם בחרב מתוך האש״ — and it would not have been fitting to use the language of 'mother' for this. But when they said 'there is a mother for the reading and for the tradition,' which is a matter transmitted through the enterprise of the oral Torah, they used 'mother' and not 'father.' And likewise here, regarding bat kol, where they used 'daughter' and not 'son,' it was required: because this voice that reaches the ears of the hearer is drawn from that voice of which it is written (Exodus 15:26): ״והיה אם שמוע תשמע לקול ה׳ אלהיך״, and likewise (Numbers 7:89): ״ושמע את הקול מדבר אליו״ — and this heard voice is called 'daughter' because it is drawn from that voice, and this is the expression bat kol. And there is yet another powerful reason why they needed to say bat kol: for the voice would descend to the ear of the hearer in the manner of the Throne, which is broad above and narrow below — for such is the form of the Throne of Glory, and such is the shape of the supernal fire: broad above and narrow below — and this is the expression bat kol, that the voice would go forth in the measure of the Throne. And this bat kol was regularly heard by the sages of the Talmud and by the pious of the generations during the time of the Second Temple, for they would make use of the bat kol after prophecy had ceased and the Urim and Tummim had ceased.<br>The second degree is Urim and Tummim, which are holy Names that point to the essence of the dim speculum (aspaklaria she-einah me'irah) through which all the prophets prophesied; they testify to its truth. And he alluded to this in the language of the Urim — which are the principal Names — whose numerical value equals the Name of the Shekhinah, as it is written (Leviticus 8:8): ״ויתן אל החשן את האורים ואת התומים״, and when you bring near the two words את האורים, then you will see אור, and in addition to it one (אחד) to testify to His unity, for this is alluded to in the ד׳ of אחד.<br>The third degree is the holy spirit (ruach ha-kodesh), and it is the tenth attribute among the attributes of the prophet, which I have already mentioned in connection with the verse (Deuteronomy 23:14 [in rabbinic versification]), ״ונשמרת״. And the nature of the holy spirit is that a person finds within himself a broadened heart, and wondrous words come to his mouth, and he declares future events, while his sensory faculties are not nullified at all; rather, he utters the words from his mouth according to what the holy spirit places in his mouth, and he himself does not know from where the words came to him.<br>The fourth degree is prophecy, which is that he declares future events with the nullification of all his sensory faculties, and he remains abstracted from matter and all his instruments, and is unified with the pure Intellect alone, and then he sees the brilliant light in visions and imaginings, whether in a dream or while awake. And there is no need to elaborate here, for I have already explained all of this at length in the book Ḥoshen Mishpat." "<b>However,</b> the view of the Ramban, of blessed memory, in his statement that it is a degree of the degrees of the holy spirit, is that the holy spirit is greater than all of them, and the order of the levels according to his view, from above to below, is as follows: holy spirit, prophecy, Urim and Tummim, bat kol. The holy spirit is the wisdom that is a spirit drawn from 'the holy' (ha-kodesh), which is the supernal source, and thus it is written (Exodus 31:3 [35:31]): ״וימלא אותו רוח אלהים בחכמה״." "<b>And likewise</b> you will find in the Book of Formation (Sefer Yetzirah): 'Another: the spirit of Elohim, living — blessed and praised be the name of the Life of the Worlds — is voice (קול), spirit (רוח), and speech (דבור), and this is the holy spirit.' He called wisdom 'the spirit of Elohim, living' and counted it first for the matter of attainment, and said that it is the holy spirit. Prophecy — its foundation is from the special Name. Urim and Tummim — among the teachings of HaShem, below prophecy and above bat kol. Bat kol — in the Shekhinah. And all the levels, all of them, are drawn from the Shekhinah. You thus find: the holy spirit, which is wisdom, is above prophecy, whose foundation is in the special Name; and prophecy is above Urim and Tummim, which are the teachings of HaShem; and Urim and Tummim are above bat kol; bat kol is the last and below all of them. And therefore no heed is paid to a bat kol to contradict the words of prophecy. And the reason we call prophecy 'holy spirit' is because it is the source from which all the levels cascade — and understand this." "<b>To Your pious man (לאיש חסידך).</b> Every High Priest from the tribe of Levi is called חסיד (pious) because he draws power from the attribute of חסד, which is the attribute of the priest, and he is the one who blesses Israel with the explicit Name, as it says (Numbers 6:23): ״כה תברכו״ — 'Thus you shall bless.' For he draws the blessing from his attribute to the tenth attribute, which is called כה, which is the attribute of judgment, as it says (Exodus 7:16–17): ״והנה לא שמעת עד כה״ — 'and behold, you have not hearkened until now (כה),' and immediately after it: ״בזאת תדע״ — 'by this you shall know.' And through this the world was blessed by the blessing of the priest. And this is what is written (Psalms 145:10): ״וחסידיך יברכוכה״ — 'and Your pious ones shall bless You' — which is read as יברכו כה — 'they shall bless כה.' And this is clear."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:30.652279278Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}